// THE LANGUAGE'S FACTS (spec/theories.md §7, docs/facts.md).
//
// A fact is an axiom schema  ∀x̄. G(x̄) → C(x̄)  added to the theory the refinement
// layer decides. Each one here is F-B: one trigger term, flat, covering every
// parameter, linear once the trigger is read as an atom. That is a LOCAL theory
// extension (Sofronie-Stokkermans, CADE 2005): instantiating on terms already
// present is sound, terminates, and is as complete as the linear procedure.
//
// `lang` is closed, so these are the same on every target. Each is TRUE of the
// integers, with a proof below, and each has a test that fails when it is
// deleted or weakened (emit/fact_test.go).
// F11, the lower half. A length counts elements, so it is a natural number.
fact len_nonneg (t ∈ Array(A)) 0 <= len(t)

// F1 and F2 — both halves of Euclidean division by a positive literal.
// For x ≥ 0 and k ≥ 1, q = ⌊x/k⌋ is the unique integer with k·q ≤ x < k·q + k,
// and truncation toward zero agrees with the floor exactly when x ≥ 0.
fact div_floor (x ∈ Int, k ∈ Int) when(0 <= x, 1 <= k) k * (x / k) <= x && x <=
k * (x / k) + (k - 1)

// F6, F7, F8 — THE REMAINDER, one law that was three encodings in Go. Truncating
// division gives a = b·q + r with |r| < |b| and r taking a's sign (integers.md §4,
// measured agreeing on all four hosts). Written as four clauses because |·| is
// not linear: two bound r by the divisor, two by the dividend. The interval layer
// splits an argument's interval at these guards and meets the clauses per cell.
fact rem_divisor_pos (a ∈ Int, b ∈ Int) when(1 <= b) 1 - b <= a % b && a % b <= b - 1

fact rem_divisor_neg (a ∈ Int, b ∈ Int) when(b <= -1) b + 1 <= a % b && a % b <= -1 - b

fact rem_dividend_nonneg (a ∈ Int, b ∈ Int) when(0 <= a) 0 <= a % b && a % b <= a

fact rem_dividend_nonpos (a ∈ Int, b ∈ Int) when(a <= 0) a <= a % b && a % b <= 0

// F9 — A MASK. In two's complement `&` only clears bits, so every bit of a&b is a
// bit of each operand. A non-negative operand has its sign bit clear, hence so
// does the result, and a&b ≤ that operand — whatever the other operand is,
// negative included. Two clauses, one per operand; the interval layer meets them
// where both hold, which is the min(a, b) bound.
fact and_left (a ∈ Int, b ∈ Int) when(0 <= a) 0 <= `&`(a, b) && `&`(a, b) <= a

fact and_right (a ∈ Int, b ∈ Int) when(0 <= b) 0 <= `&`(a, b) && `&`(a, b) <= b
